The check is the same `isTTY` property from the last lesson, applied to `process.stdin` instead of `process.stdout`:

```js
import prompts from 'prompts';

async function resolveInvoiceId(idArg, invoices) {
  if (idArg) return idArg;
  if (!process.stdin.isTTY) {
    console.error('Error: invoice ID required (no ID given and no terminal to prompt).');
    process.exit(1);
  }
  const { id } = await prompts({
    type: 'select',
    name: 'id',
    message: 'Which invoice?',
    choices: invoices.map((i) => ({ title: i.summary, value: i.id })),
  });
  return id;
}
```

If `process.stdin.isTTY` is falsy — piped input, a CI runner, a cron job — the tool fails immediately with a specific, actionable error instead of silently waiting for an answer that will never come. That failure is far more useful than a three-hour timeout, because it tells the person debugging it exactly what was missing.

```js
program
  .command('delete-client <name>')
  .option('-y, --yes', 'Skip the confirmation prompt')
  .action(async (name, options) => {
    if (!options.yes) {
      if (!process.stdin.isTTY) {
        console.error('Error: --yes is required to delete a client from a non-interactive session.');
        process.exit(1);
      }
      const { confirmed } = await prompts({
        type: 'confirm',
        name: 'confirmed',
        message: `Delete client "${name}"? This can't be undone.`,
      });
      if (!confirmed) process.exit(1);
    }
    await deleteClient(name);
    console.log(`Deleted client "${name}".`);
  });
```

Three cases are all handled: a human at a terminal without `--yes` gets prompted; a human (or script) that already passed `--yes` skips straight to the action; and a non-interactive session without `--yes` fails immediately and explicitly, rather than hanging on a prompt nobody can answer. That third branch is what turns "invtrack sometimes just hangs" into "invtrack told me exactly what flag I forgot."
